WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.563
One customer asks for a EURUSD price.

2
00:00:05.563 --> 00:00:07.807
Three providers can quote it.

3
00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:09.355
You want the lowest.

4
00:00:09.567 --> 00:00:14.075
One after another, their times add up: 620 milliseconds.

5
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:19.533
Start all three together with async, and you wait only for the slowest: 300.

6
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:23.913
Give the request a deadline: 250 milliseconds.

7
00:00:24.133 --> 00:00:25.756
Where does the timeout go?

8
00:00:25.756 --> 00:00:28.627
Around the whole fan-out, or around each call?

9
00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:33.823
Around the whole fan-out, the deadline cancels everything.

10
00:00:33.823 --> 00:00:37.969
Northbank and Fjord had answered, and both answers are thrown away.

11
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:44.261
Around each call, with withTimeoutOrNull, only Eastgate is cut off.

12
00:00:44.261 --> 00:00:45.350
It becomes null.

13
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:48.957
The answers that arrived still count, and Fjord wins.

14
00:00:49.867 --> 00:00:52.841
Now a rougher run, with no tight deadline.

15
00:00:52.841 --> 00:00:55.320
Northbank fails at 50 milliseconds.

16
00:00:55.533 --> 00:00:59.515
Inside coroutineScope, the three calls are one unit of work.

17
00:00:59.515 --> 00:01:03.099
One failure cancels the others, and the request fails.

18
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.727
A quote needs any one answer.

19
00:01:05.727 --> 00:01:08.663
supervisorScope lets each call fail alone.

20
00:01:08.867 --> 00:01:12.917
At 100 milliseconds, Fjord's own HTTP client gives up.

21
00:01:12.917 --> 00:01:18.693
It throws TimeoutCancellationException, which is a CancellationException too.

22
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:24.283
Rethrow every cancel, the usual rule, and Fjord's timeout ends your whole request.

23
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:27.982
So ask first: was my own coroutine cancelled?

24
00:01:27.982 --> 00:01:32.572
ensureActive throws only if it was, say because the client hung up.

25
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:33.986
Here it wasn't.

26
00:01:33.986 --> 00:01:38.253
Fjord just has no answer, and Eastgate answers at 300.

27
00:01:40.367 --> 00:01:41.067
Last case.

28
00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:43.027
All three miss the deadline.

29
00:01:43.027 --> 00:01:46.808
Waiting breaks the deadline, and failing helps nobody.

30
00:01:47.033 --> 00:01:51.578
So serve the last good quote, marked stale, and let the client decide.

31
00:01:51.578 --> 00:01:53.916
Only live answers refresh the cache.

32
00:01:54.833 --> 00:01:59.021
Here it is in Kotlin, with the Java version in Reactor below it.

33
00:02:00.833 --> 00:02:01.432
The rule.

34
00:02:01.432 --> 00:02:08.420
Start every call together, give each the deadline, let each fail alone, and rethrow only your own cancel.

35
00:02:08.633 --> 00:02:09.726
Check yourself.

36
00:02:09.726 --> 00:02:12.638
You rethrow every CancellationException.

37
00:02:12.638 --> 00:02:15.915
Fjord's client times out at 100 milliseconds.

38
00:02:15.915 --> 00:02:18.900
What happens to Eastgate's answer at 300?

39
00:02:21.633 --> 00:02:22.811
It never counts.

40
00:02:22.811 --> 00:02:28.111
The request fails at 100 milliseconds with TimeoutCancellationException.
